WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.500
- One of the great things about
adopting an infinite mindset

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.320
is I've stopped looking at things

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.652
in terms of right, wrong, good, bad.

00:00:07.652 --> 00:00:11.900
But taking things more in
terms of context, right?

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.930
So I'm not against finite games.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:14.930
If you're in a finite game,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.970
you have to play with a finite mindset.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.950
But if you're in an infinite game,

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.470
you have to play with an infinite mindset.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.090
And with an infinite game, of
course there are finite games.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.070
Of course, you have a product to make

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.510
that has to get out on time

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:27.640
and there's a beginning, middle, and end,

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.120
and you have to meet your deadlines.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.670
And we get these products out on time

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:32.960
and meet our finite objectives

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.133
in order to advance the
infinite vision, right?

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.410
So for example, I'll give
you a perfect example.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.130
So we're constantly told

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.700
to know our strengths and
our weaknesses, right?

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.780
Build on your strengths,
mitigate your weaknesses.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:47.770
Are they strengths?

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:48.830
Are they weaknesses?

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.950
Or is it all context, right?

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.230
So if you are very self-confident,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.540
sometimes you're arrogant.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.150
So in other words, a strength
can actually have liability,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.260
but our weaknesses
sometimes have strengths.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.810
I'll give you a perfect example.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.410
I am chronically disorganized.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.420
I mean, it's embarrassing, right?

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.110
And this I've always believed
was a horrible weakness.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.550
And I beat myself up for it.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.130
And I say how I need to fix this.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.880
And I try all these systems
that don't really work,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:14.810
because you have to be organized

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.210
to have an organized system.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.060
I'll give you a perfect example

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.530
of just how disorganized I am.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.650
As a young entrepreneur,
when I was just starting out,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.950
I would go to a networking event,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.720
and of course you collect business cards.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.233
And a good entrepreneur,

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:29.340
an organized entrepreneur,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.420
would take a business card from a prospect

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.440
if somebody goes, "I think
you're great, here's my card."

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.150
And they would have sent
an email on the cab home

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.620
and followed up the next day and be like,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.730
would love to set up a meeting

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.410
and come and show you our product.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:45.150
Yahoo over here would
lose the business card

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.380
from the time I got it to
the time I got home, right?

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.653
Two weeks later, I'd find
it under a pile of things,

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.910
sort of brush off the dust bunnies.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.057
And I'd call them up and say,

00:01:57.057 --> 00:01:59.560
"Hey, remember we met at
that event two weeks ago?"

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.200
They wanted to work with me even more

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.616
'cause they thought I was busy!

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.883
Is disorganized a bad thing?

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.320
The answer is context.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.220
Sometimes, yeah, it
really is, but not always.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.320
And so now when I look at myself,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.770
or I look at the weaknesses or strengths

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.540
even of my own organization,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.410
I no longer think in terms
of right wrong, good, bad.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.210
But I rather think this is
really valuable in this context,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.710
this is really dangerous in this context.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.560
So I can mitigate, and move things around,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.470
and understand what to leverage.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.600
So it's much more about
balance and context

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:37.800
than it is about right, wrong, good, bad.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.860
And all of this has come
from learning to adopt

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.070
this infinite mindset, that
I'm never a loser or a winner.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.840
I'm just ahead and behind.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.270
Sometimes I'm ahead and sometimes behind,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.064
but I've never won and I've never lost.

